- **D-3 THE KEEP-AWAKE WRITE IS ONE DATABASE UPDATE, NOT A TEMPLATE DEPLOY.** A template deploy re-applies all of `main.bicep` (31 resources: SQL, OpenAI, vault, storage, 6 role assignments, both sites) and replaces each site's whole app-settings collection; on the demo B836 found 21 Modify and a live-only setting a template deploy would remove (`B836_STATUS.md:121`), and the what-if masks app settings (`:87`). The approval is the database change. So the write is exactly `az sql db update -g hpsm-poc-uat-rg -s <the one UAT SQL server> -n hpsmpoc --use-free-limit false --auto-pause-delay -1 -o json` (the server name read by `az sql server list -g hpsm-poc-uat-rg --query "[].name" -o tsv`: exactly one). **First** `az sql db update --help` must list both `--use-free-limit` and `--auto-pause-delay` (UNMEASURED by the drafter); if either is missing: STOP and ask (Q-4), never `az resource update`, never a hand template. After it, live = `S`'s template for the database (proved by a second what-if of `S`: the database shows NoChange or only the control's noise).
- **D-4 No app setting, vault, firewall, plan or access write, ever.** Names, value hashes and slot flags equal before and after on both UAT apps (API 35 / web 17, B971 A4), by program.
- **D-5 Order: DRY RUN -> PART P (packages) -> WAKE TEST -> PART D (database) -> KEEP-AWAKE PROOF.** The packages are free and reversible, so they go before the one-way write; the wake test proves #266 on the real sleeping database (B991's proposed follow-up, `B991_STATUS.md:185`), which is impossible after PART D. A dry-run refusal stops everything (Stamp slot 12). A database-write refusal after PART P leaves UAT with the first-click fix only (the card's option b state, no new cost): end `PARTIAL:` and Tuesday takes the recreate question to the product owner.
